  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nRelatively low confidence forecast continues as predictability\nhorizon is mainly just the next 6 to 12 hours as thunderstorm\ncomplexes push along the front and then dominate the forecast\nfor the next period of 6-12 hours.   \n\nA series of thunderstorm complexes overnight and today has\npushed the effective outflow boundary/front relatively far south\nand stabilized much of the forecast area. The rich moisture and\ninstability extends from central Indiana into southern Ohio and\nsouthern West Virginia, and repeated rounds of thunderstorms\nalong the boundary are likely to keep it from lifting back\nnorth tonight and Wednesday.\n\nAnother thunderstorm cluster is expected to brush the Ohio\nCounties and perhaps the West Virginia Counties late this\nafternoon and this evening. Severe threat is much lower due to\nstable boundary layer, but heavy rain threat will continue\nespecially in the far southwest portion of forecast area near\nZZV. \n\nWith the effective boundary gradually sagging south with each\nMCS, the axis of heaviest rain later tonight into Wednesday will\nlikely setup from northern Indiana SE into southern Ohio and may\nbe outside of our forecast area.\n\nFly in the ointment tonight is that a separate axis of\ninstability that extends from southern lower MI across Lake \nErie and brushes northwest PA. Some of the guidance develops \nscattered slow moving thunderstorms capable of producing heavy \nrainfall along the I-80 corridor tonight. This is a lower \nconfidence scenario and the lower probabilities of 2\" or more \ndoes not warrant a flash flood watch at this time. \n\nThereafter the WNW flow pattern continues with nearly daily\nthunderstorm chances with the highest probabilities Thursday and\nSunday. Low probabilities of heavy rainfall/flooding and\ndamaging wind gusts will persist in this pattern.",
